{
  "image_name": "81b2cbe9867a41ea00d758525b2a74fe.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how peptide fragments can be joined through either selenocysteine or cysteine chemistry and how a selenocysteine residue can then be selectively converted into other amino acids. On the left, two peptide segments are shown undergoing native chemical ligation: a peptide thioester or selenoester reacts with an N‑terminal Cys or Sec residue under aqueous reducing conditions. This forms an initial acyl-linked intermediate and then rearranges to give a normal peptide bond at the junction. The scheme emphasizes that sulfur and selenium can play analogous roles in this ligation process.\n\nOn the right, the figure focuses on deselenization of a Sec-containing peptide. A Sec side chain first forms a selenium-centered radical, apparently with a phosphine reagent, producing a Se‑phosphonyl radical intermediate and then an alanyl radical after selenium is removed. From that point, two outcomes are shown: hydrogen abstraction gives alanine, while oxidation with Oxone or oxygen gives serine. Overall, the image highlights selenium as a useful, temporary handle in peptide synthesis: it enables ligation chemistry and can later be erased or transformed to leave a native amino acid residue.",
